    "q-132595": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132595",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132595-a",
              "type": "math",
